          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A (left/right)
            %em dual
            to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint"} adjoint
            to the object regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}\mathcal{C}
            = succeed "." do
              %em dualizable
          %p
            Being
            %em dualizable
            may often be thought of as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category-theoretic
            notion of
            %em finiteness
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Pareigis76"}> Pareigis 1976 p. 113
            \&amp;rbrack; for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
                is dualizable in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
                with its standard
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensor product of vector spaces
                precisely if it is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional vector space
                (cf. Exp.
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FiniteDimensionalVectorSpaces"}
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
                is dualizable in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+category"} stable homotopy category
                with its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product"} smash product
                precisely if it is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+spectrum"} finite spectrum
          %p
            A more precise intuition is that an object is dualizable if its “size” is no larger than the “additivity” of the monoidal category. For instance, since
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+category"} stable homotopy category
            are finitely
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+category"} additive
          %p
            However, in a monoidal category which is not additive at all, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            (or any cartesian monoidal category), only the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            is dualizable — whereas in an “infinitely additive” monoidal category such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rel"} Rel
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SupLat"} SupLat
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Rel
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rel
            %em all
            objects are dualizable.)
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Beware that there are other notions of “dual object”, distinct from this one. See for example
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+object+in+a+closed+category"} dual object in a closed category
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:DualityInClosedCategory"} (4)
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+duals"} category with duals
          %h2#in_a_monoidal_category In a monoidal category
          %h3#definition Definition
          #DualizableObject.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              An object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{C}, \otimes, 1)
              is
              %strong dualizable
              if it has an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjoint
              when regarded as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
              in the one-object
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}\mathcal{C}
              corresponding to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}\mathcal{C}
              is called its
              %strong dual
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              and often written as
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^*
            %p
              (This notion, though not the terminology, is due to
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a{:href => "#Lindner78"} Lindner 1978
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided
              then left and right adjoints in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}\mathcal{C}
              are equivalent; otherwise one speaks of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              being
              %strong left dualizable
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %strong right dualizable
            %p Explicitly this means the following:
            %p
              A right
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} duality
              between objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝟙
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, A^\ast \in (\mathcal{C}, \otimes, \mathbb{1})
            %p consists of
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                  of the form
                #eq:EvaluationMap.maruku-equation
                  %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ev
                          %mi A
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mo *
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi 𝟙
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        ev_A
                        \\;\colon\;
                        A^\ast \otimes A \longrightarrow \mathbb{1}
                %p
                  called the
                  %em counit
                  of the duality, or the
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/evaluation+map"} evaluation map
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                  of the form
                #eq:CoEvaluationMap.maruku-equation
                  %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi i
                          %mi A
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi 𝟙
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊗
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        i_A
                        \\;\colon\;
                        \\mathbb{1}
                        \\longrightarrow
                        A \otimes A^\ast
                %p
                  called the
                  %em unit
                  or
                  %em coevaluation map
            %p such that
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangle+identity"}> triangle identity
                  ) the following
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} diagrams commute
                .maruku-equation
